# independent altera_pll (100 MHz, refclk clk_74a) rather than a re-tune of
# mf_pllbase_0002's unused 133.12 MHz outputs -- altera_pll re-solves the whole
# VCO, and the video PLL's realized frequency is a 60.000000 Hz frame contract.
#
# on the old netlist) was measured against the old file list and the old
# single-fit number from here on.
# and give back ~144 M10K blocks.  Both files are new netlist content, so this
#     CURRENT one) at the 99.01 MHz CPU request, ranked on clk_cpu because the
#     CPU has its own PLL now and clk_74a closing says nothing about it:
#       1 -> 12.960 ns (77.2 MHz)     4 -> 13.508 (74.0)
#       2 -> 12.582 ns (79.5 MHz)     5 -> 12.834 (77.9)
#       3 -> 12.765 ns (78.3 MHz)     6 -> 13.703 (73.0)
# list change invalidates this one too.  Re-sweep with

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# EXPERIMENT SWITCH — the video capture clock's 90-degree phase (core_top.v).
# Uncommenting the next line drives the platform's capture clock
# (video_rgb_clock_90 -> apf_top's `isclk` -> the scaler's `scal_clk` pad) from
# the 0-degree pixel-clock net instead of the PLL's 90-degree output: no second
# clock, no phase.  The link is DDR, so a 0-degree capture edge lands on the
# data transitions — this is expected to break the picture (see the WHY block
# build can let the panel answer the question.  Default = OFF = the proven
# hardware arrangement; if a build with it ever comes back clean, this is the
# shape to keep and outclk_1 + phase_shift1 can be deleted for good.
# set_global_assignment -name VERILOG_MACRO "VID_NO_CLOCK90"
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# THE DIAGNOSTIC SWITCH THAT USED TO BE HERE IS GONE (2026-10-15).
#
# `MC64_BOOT_PICTURE_ALWAYS` held the bring-up grid and the six status blocks on
# the link whatever `run_core` did, so one flash answered both "are the 24 RGB
# bits arriving intact" and "where did the boot stop".  The picture itself was
# removed from core_top.v (2026-10-15) because it had become a loading screen on
# the panel -- it sat there for the whole quiet window between the OS finishing a
# cartridge and this core releasing the machine -- so a switch that keeps a
# deleted picture on the link has nothing left to switch.  See
# docs/minicube64-fpga-spec.md s.14 for the removal and its consequences, and
# `dbg_boot_state` for the six boot conditions it used to draw (asserted by
# tools/boot_sim.sh; readable in any simulation build).
#
# The question that switch was for -- "is a black panel a dead link or a machine
# with nothing to draw" -- is now answered by the handshake instead: a file list
# appearing at all is the proof that clk_74a, the bridge and the package are
# good (spec s.15).  For the link itself, the panel's test is a cart whose output
# is already known: white.bin / bars.bin are on the card (see the ROM test in the
# spec).
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------



# ===========================================================================
# Source file list -- Minicube64.
#
# Everything sits in the flat rtl/ directory.  The APF framework files were
# copied here from C:/Code/Wasm4/VexiiRiscv; the stock APF versions this project
# started from are kept beside them as *.stock.bak, so nothing was lost.
# ===========================================================================

# ---- APF framework --------------------------------------------------------
set_global_assignment -name VERILOG_FILE apf_top.v
set_global_assignment -name VERILOG_FILE common.v
set_global_assignment -name VERILOG_FILE io_bridge_peripheral.v
set_global_assignment -name VERILOG_FILE io_pad_controller.v
set_global_assignment -name VERILOG_FILE mf_datatable.v
set_global_assignment -name VERILOG_FILE mf_ddio_bidir_12.v
set_global_assignment -name QIP_FILE mf_datatable.qip
set_global_assignment -name QIP_FILE mf_ddio_bidir_12.qip
# rtl/apf.qip lists these same six files. VexiiRiscv lists them individually and
# leaves the .qip commented out; same here, so the order is explicit.
#set_global_assignment -name QIP_FILE apf.qip

# ---- Minicube64 machine ---------------------------------------------------
# Quartus needs one define here. rtl/timescale.vh writes its directive as
# `timescale `TS_UNIT/`TS_PREC, and Quartus reads a `timescale before it expands
# macros: it stops with Error 10096 ("incorrect use of predefined text macro
# timescale") and rtl/cpu_6502.sv and rtl/alu_6502.sv then parse as zero design
# units. NO_TIMESCALE is config.vh's switch for exactly this, and the Tiny
# Tapeout side of this repo drives synthesis the same way. Keep the line: with it
# removed the two CPU files parse as zero design units again.
set_global_assignment -name VERILOG_MACRO "NO_TIMESCALE=1"

set_global_assignment -name SYSTEMVERILOG_FILE mc64_top.sv
set_global_assignment -name SYSTEMVERILOG_FILE mc64_ram.sv
set_global_assignment -name SYSTEMVERILOG_FILE mc64_video.sv
set_global_assignment -name SYSTEMVERILOG_FILE mc64_audio.sv
set_global_assignment -name SYSTEMVERILOG_FILE cpu_6502.sv
set_global_assignment -name SYSTEMVERILOG_FILE alu_6502.sv

# ---- APU -------------------------------------------------------------------
# rtl/apu.sv is agg23/openfpga-NES's rtl/upstream/apu.sv (Kitrinx's cycle-based
# NES APU) and mc64_top instantiates it at $0110-$0130, the window machine.c
# diverts. The three files move together:
#
#   apu.sv            the APU itself. Its three package imports are the only
#                     edit to the upstream file -- upstream resolves those names
#                     through a compilation-unit import in its ppu.sv.
#   regs_savestates.sv  MiSTer's index table, which the APU takes four indices
#                     from and reads its power-on defaults out of. Only the
#                     table is here; the framework that drives the bus is not
#                     (see the note at the bottom of this file).
#   eReg_SavestateV.v the register primitive apu.sv instantiates, in
#                     Verilog-2001 so the fit and every Verilator harness build
#                     the same definition -- Verilator cannot elaborate the
#                     VHDL entity of the same name in rtl/bus_savestates.vhd,
#                     and building the two from different sources is the split
#                     tools/lint.sh's third pass exists to prevent.
set_global_assignment -name SYSTEMVERILOG_FILE apu.sv
set_global_assignment -name SYSTEMVERILOG_FILE regs_savestates.sv
set_global_assignment -name VERILOG_FILE eReg_SavestateV.v

# ---- core_top: the APF wrapper --------------------------------------------
# rtl/core_top_template.v and rtl/core_bridge_cmd_template.v are the APF's stock
# originals and must stay OUT of the build: core_top_template.v defines a module
# also called core_top, so both in the fit is a duplicate-entity error in
# Analysis & Synthesis. They are reference material.
set_global_assignment -name VERILOG_FILE core_top.v
set_global_assignment -name VERILOG_FILE core_bridge_cmd.v

# ---- clocks ---------------------------------------------------------------
# One PLL, written in the shape of the framework's own IP. See
# rtl/mf_pll_mc64_0002.v for the settings, for the machine/pixel ratio
# (160/33 -- 22,000 pixels of 1.32 MHz is exactly 60.000000 Hz, which is the
# whole reason the pixel clock is not a whole division of the machine), and for
# the 90-degree phase. No .qip: there is nothing here for the IP upgrader to
# touch. Read the fit report's PLL section back: outclk_1 1.320000 MHz at a
# 189.394 ns shift.
set_global_assignment -name VERILOG_FILE mf_pll_mc64.v
set_global_assignment -name VERILOG_FILE mf_pll_mc64_0002.v

# ---- timing constraints ---------------------------------------------------
# apf_constraints.sdc is Analogue's and says not to edit it: it already declares
# clk_74a/clk_74b/bridge_spiclk and calls derive_pll_clocks. core_constraints.sdc
# is ours and holds only what the framework leaves out.
set_global_assignment -name SDC_FILE apf_constraints.sdc
set_global_assignment -name SDC_FILE core_constraints.sdc

# ---- save states ----------------------------------------------------------
#
# The whole stack, in the fit and in every simulation, from three files that do
# not exist upstream:
#
#   mc64_savestates.sv        what a state IS: an 8-byte header, the 64 words
#                             the framework's register bus carries, and the
#                             machine's 64 KB of RAM in address order.
#   save_state_controller.sv  the APF end: the 0x40000000 buffer the OS reads
#                             and writes, the command handshake, and the two
#                             FIFOs between clk_74a and the machine.
#   mc64_async_fifo.sv        one gray-pointer dual-clock FIFO, instantiated
#                             twice (one per direction); 256 entries, the
#                             crossing and nothing more.
#   mc64_state_store.sv       the save direction's staging buffer: the state's
#                             words land here at a word per clk_74a cycle, so
#                             the engine's copy is the freeze and the host's
#                             read of 66 KB happens after the machine is back.
#
# The MiSTer framework's own engines -- rtl/savestates.vhd, statemanager.vhd,
# bus_savestates.vhd, savestate_ui.sv, which have been in rtl/ since the port
# began -- stay OUT of this list, and that is the one deliberate divergence:
#
#   * they are VHDL, and every pre-fit check in this project is Verilator, which
#     cannot elaborate VHDL -- the same reason rtl/eReg_SavestateV.v exists as
#     the Verilog twin of bus_savestates.vhd's entity. One definition, in both
#     builds, is the rule tools/lint.sh's third pass enforces; with the state
#     engine it is also what makes the state path SIMULATABLE (see
#     sim/tb_savestate.cpp, which drives a save and a load through the APF
#     bridge and compares the machine against itself).
#   * savestates.vhd is written around a fixed six-region memory map (OAM,
#     mapper RAM, CHR, CHR-VRAM, CPU-RAM, CARTRAM) with an SDRAM arbiter on the
#     far side; this machine's whole memory is one 64 KB block RAM with a port
#     the engine can borrow, and its file format has one memory region.
#   * statemanager.vhd and savestate_ui.sv bring MiSTer's keyboard/gamepad slot
#     shortcuts and its rewind ring buffer. On the Pocket the OS owns the slots
#     and the file, and it asks the core for exactly one state at a time
#     (commands 0x00A0/0x00A4); nothing in this machine consumes a slot number
#     or a rewind.
set_global_assignment -name SYSTEMVERILOG_FILE mc64_savestates.sv
set_global_assignment -name SYSTEMVERILOG_FILE save_state_controller.sv
set_global_assignment -name SYSTEMVERILOG_FILE mc64_async_fifo.sv
set_global_assignment -name SYSTEMVERILOG_FILE mc64_state_store.sv

# ---- not wired up yet: uncomment each as it lands -------------------------
#
# rtl/data_loader.sv and rtl/data_unloader.sv are the framework's bridge
# streamers. They are built on the dcfifo megafunction and are NOT used: the
# cartridge path in core_top is its own four-phase handshake and the state path
# is the same gray-pointer FIFO pair, both choices VexiiRiscv made. Left out on
# purpose.
#
# CLOCKS are real now, in the clocks section above: 6.4 MHz machine (and the
# same rate half a cycle later for RAM port A), 1.32 MHz pixel, its 90-degree
# twin and 12.184615 MHz audio, all from clk_74a. Two things to read off the fit
# report: outclk_1 must be 1.320000 MHz -- the frame rate is that divided by
# 22,000 and nothing else makes it exactly 60 Hz -- and outclk_2's phase must be
# a quarter of the pixel period (189.394 ns).


set_global_assignment -name EDA_SIMULATION_TOOL "<None>"
set_global_assignment -name TIMEQUEST_MULTICORNER_ANALYSIS OFF
set_global_assignment -name OPTIMIZATION_MODE "BALANCED"
set_global_assignment -name OPTIMIZE_IOC_REGISTER_PLACEMENT_FOR_TIMING OFF
set_global_assignment -name TIMEQUEST_DO_REPORT_TIMING OFF